"""Linearizability checker for atomic range transactions.

A transaction is one atomic step, so a witness is a permutation of the
transactions that respects real time (A.end <= B.start forces A before B)
and replays from `initial` to `final` with every observation matching.

Method
------
Search depth first over "which transaction runs next", carrying the current
state.  Whether the rest can be completed depends only on the set of still
unscheduled transactions and on the state, so failures are memoised.

Three observations make this fast and keep it exact:

* Every operation constrains/updates exactly one key, and a scan is
  equivalent to one "key must hold this value" test per key in its range
  (a malformed scan can never succeed at all).  So a transaction's effect
  and validity factorise per key, and can be precompiled into one
  transition table per (transaction, key): symbol -> symbol, or invalid.
  The state itself is a packed integer, one field per key.

* A transaction that cannot change any key and is runnable now can always
  be moved to the front of the rest of the schedule, so such transactions
  are scheduled greedily instead of being branched on.

* If no unscheduled transaction depends on a key (neither its validity nor
  its result) but some unscheduled one still writes it, the current value of
  that key cannot influence the rest of the run: the final value is decided
  by the last writer.  Such fields are masked out of the memo key, which
  collapses large parts of the search.

Sound (never over-pruning) tests cut off hopeless branches: a key whose
final value no remaining writer can produce, and a pending transaction
whose reads no reachable value can satisfy.

Finally, any witness that is returned is replayed straight from the request
with a plain dict, exactly as the contract specifies; and if the search
finds no witness, the same question is answered once more by a search with
none of the shortcuts above, so an answer is only reported when two
independent searches agree on it.
"""

